From cb5e4f4a9ecccd16b3d997bc609c60b74802062a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:21:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 107/108] drm/vino: add the DisplayLink DL3 USB display driver

Add the USB-facing Vino driver and connect the encrypted control protocol,
the KMS engine and the video transport. One bound-device object owns the
DRM registration, the session, the revocable I/O capability, the workqueues
and the endpoint state.

Discover typed endpoints and queue authentication on a private workqueue.
Suspend, reset, disconnect and recovery quiesce producers before revoking
USB I/O, drain submitted work, unplug DRM and release the session in
dependency order.

Two dock generations are supported, each described by a profile the rest of
the driver reads rather than branches on: the Dell D6000 (17e9:6006,
DL-6xxx) with two heads over four video endpoints, and the DL-7400
(17e9:7000, DL-7000) with four connectors multiplexed over two. What
genuinely differs in code -- the initialisation sequence, per-head HDCP
framing, stream open and mode description -- is named once as a generation
rather than spelled out again at each of the ninety-odd sites that consult
it. Products outside those two ids require their own profile and hardware
validation.

Retry transient session-establishment failures and defer a head-local
discovery timeout to bounded runtime re-engagement. Keep routine protocol
and scanout tracing behind the load-time debug parameter, and make the
KUnit suite explicitly opt-in.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Assisted-by: Codex:gpt-5
Signed-off-by: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
